WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:11.340 --> 00:00:13.999
Hello everyone,
let me introduce myself.

00:00:14.024 --> 00:00:16.271
My name is Damien Scalia,
I am a professor

00:00:16.297 --> 00:00:17.838
at the Université Libre de Bruxelles,

00:00:17.863 --> 00:00:20.485
I specialize in international criminal law

00:00:20.510 --> 00:00:23.720
and international crimes.

00:00:23.745 --> 00:00:28.650
I would first like
to thank Amah and Lilianne,

00:00:28.675 --> 00:00:34.524
who kindly invited me to participate
in your seminar.

00:00:34.549 --> 00:00:36.092
Today we are going to make

00:00:36.117 --> 00:00:38.562
a rather theoretical
and short presentation

00:00:38.587 --> 00:00:41.721
of the issues at stake
in this matter

00:00:41.746 --> 00:00:43.652
mainly concerning...

00:00:43.677 --> 00:00:47.229
This is why I was called

00:00:47.254 --> 00:00:49.863
or why I was invited here:

00:00:49.888 --> 00:00:53.934
it is mainly concerning
the crimes of the French army

00:00:53.959 --> 00:00:56.731
or the crimes France committed
in Algeria.

00:00:56.756 --> 00:00:59.259
We notice that these crimes
are of two kinds.

00:00:59.284 --> 00:01:02.804
First, war crimes
or war crimes against humanity,

00:01:02.829 --> 00:01:05.453
we will see them, since
the designation is important,

00:01:05.479 --> 00:01:10.268
committed before the decolonization,
before 1962.

00:01:10.293 --> 00:01:14.849
And also crimes committed
after 1962,

00:01:14.874 --> 00:01:17.765
since we are also
going to look today

00:01:17.789 --> 00:01:23.113
at the French nuclear tests
committed in the Sahara

00:01:23.138 --> 00:01:26.461
which started before 1962,

00:01:26.486 --> 00:01:31.061
but ended after 1962.

00:01:31.086 --> 00:01:33.773
I will come back to this
in a few moments.

00:01:33.798 --> 00:01:37.158
The objective of
this little presentation

00:01:37.183 --> 00:01:43.138
will be to give an overview
of the actions that have been carried out

00:01:43.163 --> 00:01:45.604
in relation to these events.

00:01:45.630 --> 00:01:49.666
Those are events that have marked
and still do, the relations

00:01:49.691 --> 00:01:52.246
between France and Algeria.

00:01:52.271 --> 00:01:56.122
They marked the domination
of France over Algeria

00:01:56.148 --> 00:02:02.130
and the colonization
of Algeria by France.

00:02:03.518 --> 00:02:06.889
These dramatic events,
which today still have

00:02:06.914 --> 00:02:10.019
firstly medical repercussions,

00:02:10.044 --> 00:02:14.366
human repercussions as well,
but also legal ones.

00:02:14.391 --> 00:02:19.247
Therefore there are two types of events,
like I said, the criminal acts

00:02:19.272 --> 00:02:21.400
and the nuclear tests,

00:02:21.425 --> 00:02:24.244
which I don't qualify as a crime
for the moment.

00:02:24.269 --> 00:02:26.408
We will come back to it
since it is one of the questions

00:02:26.433 --> 00:02:31.279
that will be asked
for the opening at the end of the class.

00:02:31.304 --> 00:02:34.674
As far as the crimes are concerned,
it is common knowledge

00:02:34.699 --> 00:02:38.906
and I will not go back over it,
that during the events in Algeria,

00:02:38.932 --> 00:02:41.391
like France says,
or during the Algerian War,

00:02:41.416 --> 00:02:43.715
which was qualified
of armed conflict

00:02:43.740 --> 00:02:47.555
by the French Courts,
in particular,

00:02:47.579 --> 00:02:52.055
during this war,
acts of torture, murders

00:02:52.081 --> 00:02:56.015
and summary executions
were committed

00:02:56.040 --> 00:03:00.071
by the French army
in the 1950s

00:03:00.096 --> 00:03:04.003
and up until the very early 1960s.

00:03:04.028 --> 00:03:05.843
I will not go back over
the facts themselves,

00:03:05.868 --> 00:03:09.505
you know them.

00:03:09.530 --> 00:03:12.001
If ever...

00:03:12.026 --> 00:03:15.394
It could be an exercise to do
for the seminars to come,

00:03:15.419 --> 00:03:17.895
the discussion seminars.

00:03:17.920 --> 00:03:20.230
In parallel to this, nuclear tests
were conducted

00:03:20.255 --> 00:03:24.720
from 1960 onwards,

00:03:24.745 --> 00:03:27.430
in particular the first nuclear test

00:03:27.454 --> 00:03:30.176
which was carried out at Reggane
in the Sahara.

00:03:31.446 --> 00:03:34.952
These tests continued
until 1966,

00:03:34.977 --> 00:03:39.637
meaning they began
before Algerian independence,

00:03:39.662 --> 00:03:43.240
but they continued
under an agreement

00:03:43.265 --> 00:03:47.059
that was annexed
to the Evian agreements in 1962.

00:03:47.084 --> 00:03:48.872
They continued afterward,

00:03:48.897 --> 00:03:54.343
that is to say, with the agreement
of the newly decolonized Algeria.

00:03:54.368 --> 00:03:56.652
This raises a question,
an we will see it,

00:03:56.677 --> 00:03:59.127
about Algeria's responsibility,

00:03:59.152 --> 00:04:03.395
even if this agreement,
which was kept secret for a long time,

00:04:03.420 --> 00:04:09.293
implied that the Sahara,

00:04:09.318 --> 00:04:11.592
was not completely decolonized,

00:04:11.617 --> 00:04:14.406
or in other words,
that Algeria did not have

00:04:14.431 --> 00:04:18.110
complete sovereignty
over it.

00:04:19.634 --> 00:04:24.145
These nuclear tests,

00:04:24.170 --> 00:04:27.902
mainly A-bomb tests,

00:04:27.927 --> 00:04:30.343
there were 17 in total

00:04:30.368 --> 00:04:34.425
and they were recognized
very late,

00:04:34.450 --> 00:04:39.300
just as the crimes
of the Algerian army were recognized

00:04:39.325 --> 00:04:43.297
very late in the French law.

00:04:43.322 --> 00:04:48.563
I will come back to this in a few moments
because I will come back to a law,

00:04:48.588 --> 00:04:54.228
the Morin Law of 2010,
which not only recognized them, but allows

00:04:54.253 --> 00:04:58.720
the victims
of those nuclear tests,

00:04:58.745 --> 00:05:01.223
just as the victims
of the nuclear tests

00:05:01.248 --> 00:05:02.908
in French Polynesia,

00:05:02.933 --> 00:05:07.746
allows- so that Morin Law
of 2010,

00:05:07.771 --> 00:05:10.616
allows the victims
to obtain reparations, compensations.

00:05:10.641 --> 00:05:13.909
We will see
in what framework.

00:05:15.702 --> 00:05:19.360
Before proceeding to a more
in-depth analysis

00:05:19.385 --> 00:05:22.969
of our subject, which will be divided
in three parts.

00:05:22.994 --> 00:05:27.088
I will firstly talk about the crimes
themselves and what has been done,

00:05:27.113 --> 00:05:31.897
particularly before the French Courts
since 1980s.

00:05:31.922 --> 00:05:34.989
Then, I will address the question
of nuclear testing

00:05:35.014 --> 00:05:38.598
to tell you where we stand today
and what has or has not been done,

00:05:38.623 --> 00:05:41.287
before seeing what potential avenues
are possible.

00:05:41.312 --> 00:05:45.422
Those will ultimately be an opening
to the next seminars

00:05:45.447 --> 00:05:50.175
that I will follow in part
at a distance

00:05:50.200 --> 00:05:54.408
and in particular, by virtue of the class

00:05:54.433 --> 00:05:58.490
that we will have at the start of October,
where I will be answering

00:05:58.515 --> 00:06:02.976
a series of question
following today's class.

00:06:03.001 --> 00:06:08.263
Before beginning this reflexion
in three sections,

00:06:08.288 --> 00:06:13.427
I think it is important
to define two things.

00:06:13.452 --> 00:06:16.471
One, crimes against humanity
and two, war crimes.

00:06:16.496 --> 00:06:20.277
Why? Because these crimes
are at the heart of our concerns

00:06:20.302 --> 00:06:26.189
about France's behavior
in Algeria

00:06:26.214 --> 00:06:32.174
and without a definition,
even if it is only a quick one,

00:06:32.199 --> 00:06:34.601
we will not understand
the rest.

00:06:34.626 --> 00:06:36.506
So two things:
war crimes

00:06:36.531 --> 00:06:39.023
and crimes against
humanity came into being,

00:06:39.048 --> 00:06:41.940
legally speaking,
not factually,

00:06:41.965 --> 00:06:43.390
but legally speaking,

00:06:43.415 --> 00:06:46.512
came into being mainly
for the crimes against humanity

00:06:46.537 --> 00:06:50.665
and were developed mainly
for war crimes,

00:06:50.690 --> 00:06:51.857
at the time of Nuremberg.

00:06:51.882 --> 00:06:55.016
Nuremberg was
the post-war judgment.

00:06:55.041 --> 00:06:57.132
It is the judgment
that allowed the trial

00:06:57.157 --> 00:07:03.116
of about twenty
senior Nazi officials

00:07:03.141 --> 00:07:06.893
for war crimes,
crimes against humanity and at the time,

00:07:06.918 --> 00:07:11.588
crimes against peace,
which would later become

00:07:11.613 --> 00:07:13.466
the crime of aggression.

00:07:13.491 --> 00:07:17.156
War crimes were defined
at that time

00:07:17.181 --> 00:07:20.725
and will be up until 2002

00:07:20.750 --> 00:07:24.337
with the establishment of the statute
of the International Criminal Court.

00:07:24.362 --> 00:07:27.713
This Court now
prosecutes individuals

00:07:27.738 --> 00:07:29.377
who have allegedly
committed war crimes,

00:07:29.402 --> 00:07:33.463
crimes against humanity,
genocides or crimes of aggression.

00:07:33.488 --> 00:07:36.550
War crimes are defined
as acts that take place

00:07:36.575 --> 00:07:41.111
during an armed conflict.

00:07:41.136 --> 00:07:44.192
Therefore, it is a fundamental
element of context,

00:07:44.217 --> 00:07:48.103
during an armed conflict,
against protected persons

00:07:48.128 --> 00:07:49.714
or protected property.

00:07:49.739 --> 00:07:52.047
Who are these protected
persons or property?

00:07:52.072 --> 00:07:53.602
They are military

00:07:53.627 --> 00:07:56.125
who are no longer fighting
because they are wounded,

00:07:56.150 --> 00:07:58.350
because they are prisoners of war.

00:07:58.375 --> 00:08:04.343
They are the civilians who
cannot be military targets.

00:08:04.368 --> 00:08:08.921
They are people who are
specially protected:

00:08:08.946 --> 00:08:12.734
medical workers,
health workers, among others.

00:08:12.759 --> 00:08:15.898
This is a very short definition.
We should...

00:08:15.923 --> 00:08:18.405
We could go much further
in this definition,

00:08:18.430 --> 00:08:22.498
but let's retain this,
it will be sufficient for our work.

00:08:22.523 --> 00:08:25.294
Crimes against humanity,
which are very important,

00:08:25.319 --> 00:08:28.364
even more important
than the others, are defined

00:08:28.389 --> 00:08:34.326
in the Nuremberg status
in Article 6, as acts committed

00:08:34.351 --> 00:08:38.015
against the civilian population

00:08:38.039 --> 00:08:41.265
and consequently
against the civilian population.

00:08:41.289 --> 00:08:43.080
At the time of Nuremberg,

00:08:43.105 --> 00:08:46.352
they were defined
according to the context.

00:08:46.377 --> 00:08:51.510
They could only occur in the context
of an armed conflict.

00:08:51.535 --> 00:08:57.375
Today, these crimes are
possible outside of armed conflict.

00:08:57.400 --> 00:09:01.081
Therefore, massive violations
of human rights

00:09:01.106 --> 00:09:04.387
committed against the civilian population.

00:09:04.412 --> 00:09:10.218
The text says: in the context
of a widespread

00:09:10.243 --> 00:09:14.685
or systematic attack directed
against the civilian population.

00:09:14.710 --> 00:09:18.498
So here we have something important,
namely a definition

00:09:18.523 --> 00:09:23.879
that appeared in 1945 in the statute
of the Nuremberg Tribunal

00:09:23.904 --> 00:09:26.037
and that will be developed,

00:09:26.062 --> 00:09:29.224
just like the definition
of crimes against...

00:09:29.249 --> 00:09:32.590
war crimes that will be
developed until 2002

00:09:32.615 --> 00:09:34.408
and to this day.

00:09:35.686 --> 00:09:39.904
Once those two definitions have been
established, we will see that

00:09:39.929 --> 00:09:45.475
several legal or judicial
procedures have been opened,

00:09:45.500 --> 00:09:49.192
and also closed, concerning the events

00:09:49.217 --> 00:09:53.959
that interest us,
without actually succeeding.

00:09:54.332 --> 00:09:57.334
For war crimes and crimes against humanity
in the stricter sense,

00:09:57.359 --> 00:09:59.919
they have never been successful.

00:09:59.944 --> 00:10:02.498
For the war crimes
of the French army in Algeria.

00:10:02.523 --> 00:10:05.616
As for the nuclear tests,
they were not successful,

00:10:05.641 --> 00:10:07.943
but they were not
judicial as a matter of course.

00:10:09.404 --> 00:10:14.942
Note that we will be
talking here

00:10:14.967 --> 00:10:20.172
about companies,
about procedures,

00:10:20.197 --> 00:10:23.773
that have mainly targeted

00:10:24.363 --> 00:10:29.969
individuals
or the French State,

00:10:29.994 --> 00:10:35.472
but always within national procedures.

00:10:35.497 --> 00:10:40.104
meaning that procedures
of the International Court of Justice type,

00:10:40.129 --> 00:10:45.637
procedures of the UN International
Human Rights

00:10:45.662 --> 00:10:48.346
Committee type

00:10:48.371 --> 00:10:51.018
have not, to my knowledge,
been initiated

00:10:51.043 --> 00:10:53.071
or have not been successful
in any case,

00:10:54.316 --> 00:10:57.165
and I am putting aside
diplomatic procedures

00:10:57.190 --> 00:11:00.010
which do not concern our class.

00:11:02.359 --> 00:11:06.632
I do not address either,
at least not immediately,

00:11:06.657 --> 00:11:12.254
the question of the procedures opened
or potentially opened in Algeria.

00:11:12.279 --> 00:11:14.985
This will be the matter
of your research, I think,

00:11:15.010 --> 00:11:18.530
since I do not know
Algerian law well enough.

00:11:18.571 --> 00:11:21.836
I don't know Algerian
procedures too well,

00:11:21.861 --> 00:11:24.862
so we can talk about that later.

00:11:24.887 --> 00:11:27.276
So if you don't mind,
let's start

00:11:27.301 --> 00:11:33.224
with the events outside of
the nuclear tests,

00:11:33.249 --> 00:11:35.278
the events,

00:11:35.303 --> 00:11:38.632
the crimes committed
by the French army in Algeria.

00:11:38.657 --> 00:11:42.864
It is important to know that these events
were never recognized

00:11:42.889 --> 00:11:45.966
as crimes against humanity,

00:11:46.136 --> 00:11:51.156
nor, moreover, as war crimes,
and therefore

00:11:51.181 --> 00:11:55.109
they benefited from
a French amnesty law

00:11:55.134 --> 00:11:57.459
before the French Courts.

00:11:57.484 --> 00:12:01.674
A little historical reminder:

00:12:02.602 --> 00:12:07.953
in 1900, I mean, in the 1960s,

00:12:07.978 --> 00:12:11.206
in 1966 and 1968 to be exact...

00:12:11.231 --> 00:12:17.217
I mean, I'm sorry, in 1964,
France passed an amnesty law

00:12:17.242 --> 00:12:21.282
that amnestied
all offenses committed

00:12:21.307 --> 00:12:26.231
in the context of French interventions,
particularly in Algeria.

00:12:27.755 --> 00:12:30.857
This law aims...

00:12:30.882 --> 00:12:35.587
Well, it is a general
and absolute amnesty law.

00:12:35.612 --> 00:12:38.607
It extends to all offenses,

00:12:38.632 --> 00:12:41.997
which means
that it extends, at least

00:12:42.022 --> 00:12:45.203
in French law implicitly,

00:12:45.228 --> 00:12:48.491
to crimes that could be
qualified as war crimes

00:12:48.516 --> 00:12:51.593
or crimes against humanity.

00:12:51.618 --> 00:12:55.705
As early as the 1980s,

00:12:55.730 --> 00:12:59.622
two procedures,
notably in 1986-88,

00:12:59.647 --> 00:13:05.606
were initiated in France
for crimes committed:

00:13:05.631 --> 00:13:11.575
murders and torture by the French army
against Algerian nationals.

00:13:11.876 --> 00:13:15.880
These proceedings and without going into
the whole procedure,

00:13:15.905 --> 00:13:21.775
led to two very important rulings
on November 29, 1988,

00:13:21.800 --> 00:13:25.937
very important ones,
in particular the Ayyoub ruling,

00:13:27.969 --> 00:13:31.488
rulings by the Criminal Division
of the Court of Cassation,

00:13:31.513 --> 00:13:35.680
which very clearly
stated that

00:13:35.705 --> 00:13:39.536
first, the crimes themselves
were not

00:13:39.561 --> 00:13:42.977
crimes against humanity,

00:13:43.580 --> 00:13:46.723
and second, that they were
therefore potentially

00:13:46.748 --> 00:13:50.746
crimes under ordinary law,
but that these

00:13:50.771 --> 00:13:55.501
were covered by an amnesty,

00:13:55.526 --> 00:13:59.038
which was the amnesty
provided by the 1964 law.

00:13:59.063 --> 00:14:02.626
This is very surprising,
as Pierrette Poncela

00:14:02.651 --> 00:14:06.760
and Isabelle Fouchard
pointed out.

00:14:06.785 --> 00:14:09.552
It is very surprising
because the Court of Cassation

00:14:09.577 --> 00:14:14.947
had a completely different approach
in the Barbie case,

00:14:15.208 --> 00:14:21.048
which concerned crimes
committed by Klaus Barbie

00:14:21.073 --> 00:14:27.026
during the Second World War.

00:14:27.051 --> 00:14:29.100
The Court of Cassation said

00:14:29.125 --> 00:14:32.398
that the amnesty law
did not apply

00:14:32.423 --> 00:14:35.790
to the crimes committed
by Klaus Barbie,

00:14:36.846 --> 00:14:42.134
since this amnesty law
was mainly based

00:14:42.382 --> 00:14:47.521
on the Nuremberg laws,

00:14:47.546 --> 00:14:51.289
and that the crimes of the Nazis
or those associated with them

00:14:51.314 --> 00:14:53.302
were not covered
by the amnesty law.

00:14:53.327 --> 00:14:57.099
That is to say, with regard to the crimes
of the Second World War,

00:14:57.124 --> 00:15:02.374
the judges of the French High Court
set aside the amnesty law

00:15:05.484 --> 00:15:10.430
and, in a way,
made the law retroactive

00:15:10.455 --> 00:15:12.693
in order to be able
to judge Klaus Barbie.

00:15:12.718 --> 00:15:14.677
I will come back
to this retrogression

00:15:14.702 --> 00:15:16.687
because it is quite
complicated,

00:15:16.712 --> 00:15:22.401
but for the crimes in Algeria,
the Court of Cassation dismissed it.

00:15:26.197 --> 00:15:28.635
Since 1988, the Court of Cassation

00:15:28.660 --> 00:15:32.543
has never ruled differently.

00:15:32.568 --> 00:15:34.643
It has dealt with judgments

00:15:34.668 --> 00:15:37.930
concerning crimes
committed in Indochina,

00:15:37.955 --> 00:15:42.354
in another former French colony,
the Boudarel judgment.

00:15:42.379 --> 00:15:46.599
It has dealt with other cases,
notably in 2000,

00:15:46.624 --> 00:15:51.476
for crimes committed in Algeria,
in a case of May 30, 2000,

00:15:51.501 --> 00:15:54.073
and it has always gone
this direction.

00:15:54.098 --> 00:15:57.665
Namely that,

00:15:58.682 --> 00:16:02.930
on the one hand, the provisions
of the law of 1964,

00:16:02.955 --> 00:16:06.757
which relate to the imprescriptibility
of crimes against humanity,

00:16:07.221 --> 00:16:13.076
do not apply to crimes committed
in Algeria or elsewhere,

00:16:13.101 --> 00:16:17.805
with the exception of crimes
committed by the Nazis

00:16:17.830 --> 00:16:19.745
or in connection
with World War II.

00:16:19.770 --> 00:16:22.761
There is therefore imprescriptibility,
which means that crimes

00:16:22.786 --> 00:16:27.163
committed by the Nazis or in connection
with the Second World War

00:16:27.188 --> 00:16:29.718
can be prosecuted ad vitam,
but not other crimes.

00:16:29.743 --> 00:16:35.638
Moreover, and it clearly states this
in the 2000 judgment,

00:16:35.663 --> 00:16:39.750
furthermore,

00:16:43.353 --> 00:16:46.758
it considers that
the principles of legality

00:16:46.783 --> 00:16:50.027
and non-retroactivity

00:16:51.407 --> 00:16:56.912
are opposed to the application
of the new 1994 Penal Code

00:16:56.937 --> 00:16:59.774
before its entry into force.

00:16:59.799 --> 00:17:04.789
It should be noted that this new Penal Code
specifically incriminated

00:17:04.814 --> 00:17:08.231
crimes against humanity
in French law,

00:17:08.256 --> 00:17:11.435
meaning that before 1994,
there was no

00:17:11.460 --> 00:17:16.375
incrimination of crimes against humanity
in French law.

00:17:16.400 --> 00:17:18.781
This is why this Court
will consider

00:17:18.807 --> 00:17:21.421
that Algerian crimes
are common law crimes,

00:17:21.444 --> 00:17:25.998
and therefore that they can be
amnestied by the law of the 1960s.

00:17:26.303 --> 00:17:31.416
Moreover, it will consider
in these same judgments

00:17:31.442 --> 00:17:36.172
that international law,
international custom

00:17:36.198 --> 00:17:40.885
does not allow these crimes
to be qualified as crimes against humanity.

00:17:40.910 --> 00:17:43.679
Since they cannot be
qualified in national law,

00:17:43.704 --> 00:17:46.695
as we have just seen,
international law

00:17:46.721 --> 00:17:49.979
does not apply either,
this is what the Court tells us.

00:17:50.004 --> 00:17:54.964
Besides, since there is
no qualification

00:17:54.990 --> 00:17:57.603
of crimes against humanity,
the facts are prescribed

00:17:57.629 --> 00:18:03.454
by the law of 1962,
sorry, not 1964, 1962.

00:18:06.269 --> 00:18:09.515
What is at stake here
is the very definition

00:18:09.541 --> 00:18:12.340
of the crime against humanity,

00:18:12.365 --> 00:18:17.379
meaning that until 1994,
French law did not recognize

00:18:17.405 --> 00:18:19.617
the crime against humanity

00:18:22.291 --> 00:18:26.449
with the exception of crimes committed
during the Second World War

00:18:26.475 --> 00:18:30.996
by the Axis,
namely the Germans

00:18:31.021 --> 00:18:34.310
and Italians.

00:18:35.170 --> 00:18:40.427
The Court of Cassation refuses
to qualify Algerian crimes

00:18:40.453 --> 00:18:43.081
as crimes against humanity
and therefore, applies

00:18:43.106 --> 00:18:47.270
the amnesty law of 1962
to it and does not apply

00:18:47.295 --> 00:18:50.030
imprescriptibility to it,
does not apply

00:18:50.055 --> 00:18:54.482
the qualification of crimes
against humanity to it.

00:18:54.805 --> 00:18:56.947
This is something strange,
why?

00:18:56.972 --> 00:18:59.278
Well, strange...
it can be explained politically.

00:18:59.303 --> 00:19:03.567
Legally, it could have had
a completely different result.

00:19:03.592 --> 00:19:07.235
That is to say, first, it could have said
that the definition that was

00:19:07.261 --> 00:19:09.232
in Nuremberg, the one that
I spoke to you about

00:19:09.257 --> 00:19:11.327
earlier in the introduction,
applied

00:19:11.353 --> 00:19:13.022
to the crimes committed
in Algeria.

00:19:13.047 --> 00:19:14.982
Why did it applied?

00:19:15.007 --> 00:19:18.914
Because the Nuremberg statute

00:19:18.940 --> 00:19:22.489
imposed a jurisdiction

00:19:22.515 --> 00:19:24.867
for the tribunal
which stopped at war,

00:19:24.892 --> 00:19:28.272
but did not impose a definition
of crimes against humanity

00:19:28.297 --> 00:19:32.032
that stopped at crimes
committed by the Germans.

00:19:32.057 --> 00:19:33.168
That is the first thing.

00:19:33.193 --> 00:19:36.547
Here, it could have come
to another conclusion.

00:19:36.572 --> 00:19:40.354
Secondly, the Court
of Cassation tells us

00:19:40.379 --> 00:19:45.162
that since the crimes were defined in 1994,
they cannot be applied before then.

00:19:45.187 --> 00:19:49.101
However, in the Barbie judgment
of which I spoke to you earlier,

00:19:49.126 --> 00:19:54.936
it is quite clear that the Court
applies crimes against humanity

00:19:54.961 --> 00:20:00.606
before 1994, since the decision,
first, dates from before 1994;

00:20:00.632 --> 00:20:04.251
second, it applies to acts
committed in 1944,

00:20:04.426 --> 00:20:09.083
and that it could
have done it retroactively

00:20:09.108 --> 00:20:12.016
It could have done so,
in particular by basing itself,

00:20:12.041 --> 00:20:15.475
and this leads to the third argument,
on international custom,

00:20:15.500 --> 00:20:19.971
namely to say: OK, French
law may not have applied,

00:20:19.996 --> 00:20:23.099
but by virtue
of international custom,

00:20:23.124 --> 00:20:28.080
there was indeed, after 1945,
the existence of crimes against humanity

00:20:28.106 --> 00:20:32.448
or war crimes
that correspond to acts

00:20:32.473 --> 00:20:34.377
committed by the French army
in Algeria.

00:20:34.402 --> 00:20:38.683
Thus, here is a very clear
and very strict interpretation

00:20:38.708 --> 00:20:42.400
of the principle of legality,
an interpretation

00:20:42.426 --> 00:20:47.641
which is correct
to a certain extent,

00:20:47.667 --> 00:20:50.683
but which could have led
to something else,

00:20:50.709 --> 00:20:55.792
even if, in my opinion, on this point,
the Court of Cassation is quite right,

00:20:56.481 --> 00:20:59.777
and in particular in view
of the latest case law

00:20:59.802 --> 00:21:01.735
of the European Court
of Human Rights

00:21:01.760 --> 00:21:06.828
with which France
must comply.

00:21:07.312 --> 00:21:10.554
Moreover, on the question
of the amnesty

00:21:10.579 --> 00:21:14.617
which the Court takes
for granted with the 1962 law,

00:21:18.608 --> 00:21:23.272
it is clear that if in 62,
this amnesty could apply

00:21:23.297 --> 00:21:26.258
to these crimes, today,
we know very well,

00:21:26.283 --> 00:21:28.663
in particular under international law

00:21:28.688 --> 00:21:32.255
and international criminal law,
that amnesty

00:21:32.835 --> 00:21:37.352
is not acceptable
or at least for international crimes.

00:21:37.377 --> 00:21:40.071
So we have seen
very clearly appear

00:21:40.096 --> 00:21:44.593
for some twenty years now
in the jurisprudence

00:21:44.619 --> 00:21:46.285
of the European Court
of Human Rights,

00:21:46.310 --> 00:21:48.551
in the jurisprudence
of the Inter-American Court

00:21:48.576 --> 00:21:51.147
of Human Rights
and in the jurisprudence

00:21:51.173 --> 00:21:56.061
of the international criminal courts,
the rejection of the amnesty.

00:21:56.087 --> 00:21:59.972
Therefore, the French
high court

00:21:59.998 --> 00:22:05.355
could have accepted
that this amnesty was not valid.

00:22:05.589 --> 00:22:10.117
This raises a question
of the retroactivity of the non-amnesty.

00:22:10.142 --> 00:22:14.416
This is where I refer you
to the previous argument,

00:22:14.441 --> 00:22:18.585
to say that this argument could also
have been evacuated

00:22:18.610 --> 00:22:20.650
rather quickly.

00:22:20.740 --> 00:22:24.901
There you have it for the crimes
committed in Algeria

00:22:24.927 --> 00:22:30.702
and the way they were treated
by the French courts.

00:22:31.196 --> 00:22:33.222
Let's move on, if you will,
to our second point,

00:22:33.247 --> 00:22:34.937
namely the nuclear tests.

00:22:34.962 --> 00:22:38.454
As far as the nuclear tests
are concerned,

00:22:39.820 --> 00:22:43.970
you should know
that they began in 1960,

00:22:43.996 --> 00:22:48.836
that as early as 1959, there was
a resolution of the United Nations

00:22:48.862 --> 00:22:53.928
General Assembly, resolution 42/80,
which asked France

00:22:53.953 --> 00:22:56.950
not to carry out these nuclear tests.

00:22:56.976 --> 00:23:01.553
France expressly refused
to comply with this resolution,

00:23:01.578 --> 00:23:06.896
claiming that the British,
American and Russian arsenal

00:23:06.922 --> 00:23:09.885
was such that it was necessary
to protect itself.

00:23:09.910 --> 00:23:13.565
To protect itself, it was necessary
to conduct nuclear tests

00:23:13.590 --> 00:23:17.129
to create this nuclear bomb.

00:23:17.154 --> 00:23:21.472
In addition, France maintained
at the time an argument

00:23:21.497 --> 00:23:24.269
that the radioactive fallout
would not be significant.

00:23:24.294 --> 00:23:30.278
We saw the result, cancers,
diseases, deaths afterward.

00:23:30.304 --> 00:23:32.539
We need to remember
that at the time

00:23:32.564 --> 00:23:36.376
there were no treaties
prohibiting those nuclear tests.

00:23:36.401 --> 00:23:41.542
There were also no customs
prohibiting those nuclear tests.

00:23:41.567 --> 00:23:46.988
Certainly, some doctrines
consider that those nuclear tests

00:23:47.013 --> 00:23:51.659
are a violation of the convention
that represses genocide,

00:23:51.684 --> 00:23:56.801
the 1948 Convention,
or of the International Covenant

00:23:56.827 --> 00:24:00.972
on Civil and Political Rights
that was being drafted at the time.

00:24:00.997 --> 00:24:03.362
It seems rather
unrealistic to me.

00:24:03.387 --> 00:24:07.130
One question that has not been asked is:
can we consider

00:24:07.156 --> 00:24:11.606
that those nuclear tests
are crimes against humanity?

00:24:12.839 --> 00:24:14.748
We will have to think
about this.

00:24:14.774 --> 00:24:16.463
It was not asked like that.

00:24:16.489 --> 00:24:22.489
However, I think that
one could support it,

00:24:22.515 --> 00:24:24.563
or at least try to support it.

00:24:24.795 --> 00:24:30.684
Note, however, that if there was
no criminal procedure

00:24:30.710 --> 00:24:33.816
in the strict sense of the term,
there was recognition

00:24:33.841 --> 00:24:36.773
of those nuclear tests
and the repercussions

00:24:36.798 --> 00:24:40.081
of those nuclear tests
on the victims.

00:24:40.912 --> 00:24:44.173
This recognition took place
and its repercussions,

00:24:44.198 --> 00:24:47.205
this recognition of the repercussions
took place through a law,

00:24:47.231 --> 00:24:52.620
the Morin Law of 2010,
and this law allows

00:24:52.646 --> 00:24:55.580
the victims of French
nuclear tests,

00:24:55.605 --> 00:24:58.141
whether in Algeria
or in French Polynesia,

00:24:58.167 --> 00:25:01.707
to ask for reparation.

00:25:02.610 --> 00:25:07.517
This law allows them to ask for reparation
with something quite interesting.

00:25:07.542 --> 00:25:11.961
First, it reverses
the proof of causality.

00:25:11.986 --> 00:25:15.565
The law clearly states
that if people prove

00:25:15.590 --> 00:25:19.085
that they are ill
and that they were present

00:25:19.110 --> 00:25:24.664
in the surroundings of the nuclear tests
carried out by France,

00:25:24.690 --> 00:25:27.808
already in the Sahara,

00:25:29.008 --> 00:25:33.853
then the victims can have
access to reparations.

00:25:33.878 --> 00:25:37.748
It is not a criminal conviction,
they are reparations,

00:25:37.773 --> 00:25:41.856
it is mainly
financial reparations,

00:25:41.881 --> 00:25:47.376
but the law says it within a very strict
framework, namely that there must be

00:25:47.401 --> 00:25:49.775
particular diseases.

00:25:49.801 --> 00:25:51.615
These diseases are
as follows.

00:25:51.641 --> 00:25:54.615
They are listed in a decree,

00:25:54.641 --> 00:25:58.349
decree 2014-1049

00:25:58.374 --> 00:26:01.030
which follows on
from the Morin law of 2010.

00:26:01.055 --> 00:26:03.663
These are leukemia,

00:26:03.688 --> 00:26:07.457
but not chronic
lymphoid leukemia,

00:26:07.483 --> 00:26:12.975
since the doctors considered
that it was not radio induced,

00:26:13.000 --> 00:26:16.701
meaning that it did not
come from radioactivity,

00:26:17.938 --> 00:26:22.565
myelodysplasias,
breast cancers,

00:26:22.591 --> 00:26:27.886
thyroid cancers for an exposure
during the period of growth,

00:26:27.912 --> 00:26:33.456
as a child, skin cancers,
but not malignant melanomas,

00:26:33.481 --> 00:26:36.450
lung cancers,
colon cancers,

00:26:36.475 --> 00:26:39.273
salivary gland cancers,
etc.

00:26:39.298 --> 00:26:43.373
I refer you to the text
of the decree 2014-1049.

00:26:43.398 --> 00:26:48.722
That is, for these diseases,
there is a reversal of causality.

00:26:48.747 --> 00:26:52.489
If there are no such diseases,
if there are other diseases

00:26:52.514 --> 00:26:55.750
and a priori, there are other diseases
that were induced

00:26:55.776 --> 00:27:00.155
by exposure
to radiation,

00:27:00.181 --> 00:27:02.999
then there is no
presumption of causality,

00:27:03.025 --> 00:27:04.830
which does not mean that
there can be no compensation.

00:27:04.855 --> 00:27:08.081
This means that it is necessary to prove
the link, which is complicated

00:27:08.106 --> 00:27:12.793
because the law was there
precisely to frame this causality.

00:27:13.549 --> 00:27:18.724
What is even more complicated
is that if French military veterans

00:27:18.750 --> 00:27:21.126
were able to be compensated
because they had paper

00:27:21.152 --> 00:27:23.129
showing that they were
indeed present on the spot,

00:27:23.154 --> 00:27:25.307
because there are
army records,

00:27:25.332 --> 00:27:30.600
the local civilian populations have
difficulty proving this.

00:27:30.988 --> 00:27:34.473
Since they have a hard time proving that,
well there is no compensation.

00:27:34.498 --> 00:27:38.832
There is only one known case
of a local civilian woman

00:27:38.857 --> 00:27:42.795
who was compensated
for the death of her husband

00:27:42.820 --> 00:27:44.703
a few decades later,

00:27:45.775 --> 00:27:47.335
but that's the only case.

00:27:47.360 --> 00:27:50.606
It is the only case
and it is quite unique.

00:27:51.064 --> 00:27:55.006
We could talk about it again
if you want a little later.

00:27:55.898 --> 00:28:00.104
So, reparations if we can prove
that we were present,

00:28:00.236 --> 00:28:04.196
in the case of particular diseases,
but nothing more.

00:28:04.806 --> 00:28:08.316
That is all
for the nuclear tests.

00:28:08.341 --> 00:28:11.929
Some avenues for reflection that
we will discuss again during the class

00:28:11.954 --> 00:28:16.871
and the questions and answers
at the beginning of October.

00:28:17.562 --> 00:28:20.479
First, a question
of qualification.

00:28:21.478 --> 00:28:26.251
Today we must try,

00:28:26.277 --> 00:28:27.859
not to qualify the crimes.

00:28:27.884 --> 00:28:30.274
Thus, the first part
that we talked about, the events,

00:28:30.299 --> 00:28:33.780
because these are crimes
against humanity, without doubt,

00:28:33.805 --> 00:28:36.540
even if the Court of Cassation
has ruled that out,

00:28:36.565 --> 00:28:39.050
but the question must be asked
whether nuclear tests

00:28:39.076 --> 00:28:41.811
are or can be qualified
as crimes against humanity.

00:28:41.836 --> 00:28:43.585
For that,
we must take up

00:28:43.611 --> 00:28:45.347
the statute of
the International Criminal Court,

00:28:45.372 --> 00:28:47.956
which gives the law
in force today,

00:28:47.982 --> 00:28:52.384
to see if we can qualify this
as a crime against humanity.

00:28:53.315 --> 00:28:56.583
Note that even if we were able to do this,
it does not mean,

00:28:56.608 --> 00:29:01.254
given the arguments
of the Court of Cassation,

00:29:01.280 --> 00:29:04.874
that we could assert this
before the French courts.

00:29:04.899 --> 00:29:07.148
But in any case,
it would already be a point,

00:29:07.203 --> 00:29:11.236
of crimes against humanity,
perhaps of war crimes.

00:29:11.261 --> 00:29:12.273
Why?

00:29:12.298 --> 00:29:15.284
Because since Nuremberg,
war crimes

00:29:15.309 --> 00:29:19.678
against the environment
have been elaborated,

00:29:19.704 --> 00:29:21.567
have been incriminated.

00:29:21.593 --> 00:29:25.177
One might wonder if
the nuclear tests in the Sahara

00:29:25.202 --> 00:29:28.349
are not crimes against
the environment

00:29:28.374 --> 00:29:33.108
or if there is something to be done here,
there is a little bit of digging to be done.

00:29:34.650 --> 00:29:40.053
Moreover, we must work again,
we must research,

00:29:40.079 --> 00:29:41.364
we must do research
on this question

00:29:41.389 --> 00:29:43.252
of imprescriptibility.

00:29:43.650 --> 00:29:45.061
Why?

00:29:45.087 --> 00:29:49.788
Because this imprescriptibility
is questionable,

00:29:50.239 --> 00:29:52.149
is questionable because

00:29:52.175 --> 00:29:57.131
if we manage

00:29:57.157 --> 00:29:59.936
to qualify the different crimes
as crimes against humanity,

00:29:59.961 --> 00:30:04.886
a priori, we should be able to say
that these crimes are imprescriptible,

00:30:04.911 --> 00:30:10.393
which would mean that
if at the time of their acts,

00:30:10.418 --> 00:30:12.777
they were already provided for
by an international law,

00:30:12.802 --> 00:30:14.080
which was the case,

00:30:14.106 --> 00:30:16.278
they should not be able
to be prescribed.

00:30:16.303 --> 00:30:19.956
Penally speaking, we have seen
that this does not lead to much.

00:30:19.982 --> 00:30:25.965
On the other hand,
one could wonder whether

00:30:25.991 --> 00:30:30.428
international or national authorities,
and I will come to this,

00:30:30.454 --> 00:30:34.315
it would be worthwhile

00:30:34.819 --> 00:30:38.281
to rework this question
of imprescriptibility.

00:30:39.569 --> 00:30:42.903
Finally,

00:30:43.594 --> 00:30:45.792
and this is the most
important thing,

00:30:45.818 --> 00:30:48.283
we must think about
several procedures.

00:30:49.198 --> 00:30:52.410
First of all, as far as
procedures are concerned,

00:30:52.436 --> 00:30:54.632
procedures in France,

00:31:00.541 --> 00:31:03.715
apart from the question
of reparations,

00:31:03.740 --> 00:31:08.634
but even then it is necessary to find
proof in relation to the nuclear tests,

00:31:08.659 --> 00:31:10.841
there is little chance
of success.

00:31:11.165 --> 00:31:17.146
Therefore, the procedures in France
should focus mainly

00:31:17.172 --> 00:31:19.620
on, first, the nuclear tests,
qualifying them

00:31:19.646 --> 00:31:21.189
as crimes against humanity.

00:31:21.214 --> 00:31:24.886
That, I think, has not
been researched enough

00:31:24.912 --> 00:31:26.523
Second,

00:31:27.387 --> 00:31:31.491
see civil reparation
procedures

00:31:31.517 --> 00:31:34.088
by the Algerian victims,
by providing proof

00:31:34.113 --> 00:31:35.948
that they were indeed present.

00:31:35.973 --> 00:31:39.849
Still at the national level,
another possible procedure,

00:31:39.874 --> 00:31:45.284
and this is research
that must be done,

00:31:45.310 --> 00:31:47.198
that you must do,

00:31:47.600 --> 00:31:50.734
is to see if it is not possible
to lodge a complaint,

00:31:50.759 --> 00:31:56.692
to bring before the Algerian civil judge,
the events,

00:31:56.718 --> 00:31:59.673
the cases, the crimes,
the nuclear tests,

00:31:59.699 --> 00:32:05.527
thus, to bring a complaint
against the French State,

00:32:05.553 --> 00:32:07.493
but before
the Algerian courts,

00:32:07.518 --> 00:32:09.373
against French nationals,

00:32:09.399 --> 00:32:13.026
but before the Algerian courts.

00:32:13.051 --> 00:32:14.820
I do not know Algerian Law.

00:32:14.845 --> 00:32:16.746
I do not know Algerian procedures.

00:32:16.771 --> 00:32:20.732
Since the French
can judge by universal jurisdiction

00:32:20.757 --> 00:32:24.145
or by other links of connection,
foreigners on its territory,

00:32:24.170 --> 00:32:26.660
why Algeria could not?

00:32:26.685 --> 00:32:29.654
Here we are going to take
into account other problems,

00:32:29.680 --> 00:32:32.436
political problems,
diplomatic problems,

00:32:32.461 --> 00:32:35.001
but legally, it might be worth it.

00:32:35.027 --> 00:32:40.725
Then, at the international level, it is clear
that the international criminal tribunals,

00:32:40.751 --> 00:32:44.234
the International Criminal Court
are not bodies

00:32:44.260 --> 00:32:45.480
which have jurisdiction.

00:32:45.506 --> 00:32:48.196
So I have not mentioned them
and I exclude them.

00:32:48.222 --> 00:32:50.106
On the other hand,
one could wonder

00:32:50.132 --> 00:32:53.981
if something could not be done
before the European Court of Human Rights

00:32:54.006 --> 00:32:56.970
for nuclear tests

00:32:56.995 --> 00:32:58.656
as for international crimes.

00:32:58.682 --> 00:33:04.642
For that we must review a little
what is the jurisprudence of the Court

00:33:04.668 --> 00:33:10.098
and then perhaps

00:33:10.124 --> 00:33:13.058
in a much less
aggressive way,

00:33:13.084 --> 00:33:17.188
but maybe more diplomatic,
to try to go also

00:33:17.213 --> 00:33:20.083
before the Human Rights Committee.

00:33:20.108 --> 00:33:23.972
For these jurisdictions,
for these instances,

00:33:24.273 --> 00:33:27.022
it could be interesting
to carry out procedures

00:33:27.047 --> 00:33:30.148
and I come back to my previous point,
in Algeria to say

00:33:30.173 --> 00:33:31.474
that they are not effective,

00:33:31.499 --> 00:33:33.181
because they will
certainly not be successful,

00:33:33.206 --> 00:33:36.201
and to go before
the United Nations committees.

00:33:36.567 --> 00:33:39.123
I suggest you keep
this in mind.

00:33:39.148 --> 00:33:42.844
We will talk about this again
in a few days or weeks,

00:33:42.869 --> 00:33:45.286
depending on when
you see me on video.

00:33:45.311 --> 00:33:49.161
Thank you for your attention
and I'll see you soon.

